WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.912
Meet a patient who needs an appointment, and a clinic team working together to provide care.

00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:12.960
Watch how Sahayak connects the entire visit: booking, arrival, consultation, digital instructions and feedback.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.440
Each step updates the same patient journey, so the team can see what happens next.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.087
It is late in the evening, or the patient is somewhere they cannot make a call.

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:28.130
They start with a simple hello.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:35.280
Here, the conversation opens the clinic’s booking flow, making it easier to begin without waiting for someone to answer the phone.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.408
The patient asks to see the doctor.

00:00:39.408 --> 00:00:43.591
The assistant gathers the booking details through a short conversation.

00:00:43.591 --> 00:00:51.120
The clinic team spends less time answering routine appointment questions, while the doctor can stay focused on consultations.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.551
Is this a first visit or a follow-up?

00:00:55.551 --> 00:00:59.589
The patient answers, and that information stays with the booking.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:06.050
The care team gets useful context before meeting the patient, rather than starting from scattered messages.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.935
The assistant checks the clinic’s available times and presents the choices.

00:01:11.935 --> 00:01:18.750
The patient can choose a convenient appointment, while the doctor’s calendar remains the source for availability.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.244
The patient chooses a time.

00:01:22.244 --> 00:01:26.373
The assistant brings the booking details together for confirmation.

00:01:26.373 --> 00:01:31.420
This simple exchange turns an enquiry into a clear appointment request.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.697
The patient confirms, and the appointment appears in the clinic’s records.

00:01:36.697 --> 00:01:44.480
The clinic team can now see who is expected and when, without copying details from a chat into a separate diary.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.622
The patient chooses to receive clinic updates.

00:01:48.622 --> 00:01:54.086
Their preference is saved with the visit, so future communication follows their choice.

00:01:54.086 --> 00:01:59.550
The clinic team can manage the visit and communication from the same place.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:04.572
Now the doctor is running fifteen minutes late.

00:02:04.572 --> 00:02:09.351
One update identifies the affected appointments and prepares revised times.

00:02:09.351 --> 00:02:16.040
Instead of repeating the same explanation, an authorised team member reviews one organised change.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.716
The change is confirmed and the appointment time updates.

00:02:20.716 --> 00:02:26.895
The revised booking supplies the details for the patient’s notice: what changed and when to arrive.

00:02:26.895 --> 00:02:33.460
This is how a schedule change becomes a useful message, with less coordination for the clinic team.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.726
The patient arrives and checks in against the existing booking.

00:02:38.726 --> 00:02:40.758
Their visit is already recognised.

00:02:40.758 --> 00:02:47.670
The reception team can distinguish an expected appointment from someone who is now waiting to be seen.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.385
Payment is recorded and a queue token is created.

00:02:52.385 --> 00:02:55.900
Arrival, the payment record and the queue are connected.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:02.930
The clinic team can see who is ready next, while the visit’s payment information stays in one place.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:08.168
While waiting, the patient adds a question they want to discuss.

00:03:08.168 --> 00:03:10.370
That concern stays with the visit.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:16.610
It gives the doctor a useful starting point and helps the patient remember what mattered to them.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:22.163
The patient is called and the doctor begins the consultation.

00:03:22.163 --> 00:03:26.951
The queue updates with the visit, keeping the clinic team informed.

00:03:26.951 --> 00:03:33.480
Booking information and the patient’s concern are available together, helping the consultation start with context.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.857
A draft note brings the consultation text into a structured form.

00:03:38.857 --> 00:03:41.515
The doctor can correct it before signing.

00:03:41.515 --> 00:03:47.590
This reduces repeated typing and makes it easier to leave a clear record of the visit.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:53.013
The doctor reviews the note, signs it and approves the instructions.

00:03:53.013 --> 00:04:01.460
The doctor stays in control of the clinical content, while Sahayak organises the record and prepares the next steps for the patient.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.946
The approved instructions now have a private patient link.

00:04:05.946 --> 00:04:13.248
The clinic team can share this copy with the patient, so their next steps are easy to find after leaving.

00:04:13.248 --> 00:04:18.360
Patients can return to their instructions without asking the team for another paper copy.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.874
The doctor finishes the visit.

00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:26.039
The saved clinic setting schedules the feedback invitation automatically.

00:04:26.039 --> 00:04:34.370
Completing the consultation also starts the next stage of care, without creating a separate follow-up task by hand.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:40.482
Here is the message prepared for the patient, with a private feedback link.

00:04:40.482 --> 00:04:46.527
The completed visit supplies the context, and the clinic’s saved timing determines when it is due.

00:04:46.527 --> 00:04:52.950
For WhatsApp, the message’s delivery status is where the clinic team checks whether it reached the patient.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.990
The patient says the next steps need a clearer explanation.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.678
Their answer is saved against this visit.

00:05:00.678 --> 00:05:07.590
The care team can hear about a concern that might otherwise remain unspoken after the patient goes home.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:13.827
The concern is assigned for follow-up and appears as work to address.

00:05:13.827 --> 00:05:21.383
The responsible team member can see who needs attention and why, with clinical questions directed to the doctor.

00:05:21.383 --> 00:05:26.840
Feedback becomes a practical opportunity to clarify instructions and improve the patient’s experience.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.836
This is the patient’s digital copy of the approved instructions.

00:05:31.836 --> 00:05:36.771
They can read it again, print it or save it as a PDF.

00:05:36.771 --> 00:05:42.085
From the first message to the information they take home, the visit stays connected.

00:05:42.085 --> 00:05:46.640
For the whole clinic team, that means less repetition and clearer follow-through.
